    [BugFix][KVTransfer] Report Mooncake HMA receive failures by request (#18823)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Report P2P Mooncake receive failures through
    `KVConnectorTransferResults.failed_recving` when the logical cache has
    multiple groups or requires HMA. Flat block IDs do not identify the
    affected request across independent cache groups; vLLM 0.31 rejects such
    reports and can terminate Decode EngineCore after a receive failure.
    
    Preserve the existing block-level failure reporting and valid-prefix
    recovery for ordinary non-HMA caches with one logical group. Select the
    reporting mode from the logical cache configuration and HMA state
    **before** registration expands `_is_hma_required` for group-aware wire
    transfers: splitting one shared block table into several transfer groups
    must not change its recovery semantics.
    
    Retain each failed request until all local pull/reformat tasks have
    finished, then return its failure together with receive completion.
    Delegate the connector's `get_transfer_results()` to the worker so
    upstream receives the failure IDs; keep the legacy `get_finished()`
    tuple API available. No scheduler changes are needed.
    
    This follows the request-level HMA / block-level non-HMA split in
    [upstream vLLM #56855](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/56855),
    with an explicit check for multiple logical groups to satisfy the 0.31
    scheduler guard. This is a separate fix from the release upgrade in
    #18229 and applies on the current Ascend main base.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    On the affected asynchronous receive-failure path, upstream can apply
    the configured `fail` or `recompute` policy to the request instead of
    rejecting an ambiguous block-level report. Successful transfers and
    ordinary non-HMA single-group block-level recovery retain their existing
    behavior. This does not repair transport/backend configuration problems.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    CPU validation in an isolated A3-host container with no NPU device
    mappings and no network:
    
    ```bash
    python -m pytest -q --tb=short \
      tests/ut/distributed/kv_transfer/kv_p2p/test_mooncake_connector.py \
      tests/ut/distributed/kv_transfer/kv_p2p/test_remote_prefill_lifecycle.py \
      tests/ut/kv_offload
    bash format.sh ci
    ```
    
    - Before rebase, the two connector/lifecycle target suites: **192
    passed, 14 skipped on each** of verified vLLM main `c4df37d`, vLLM
    0.31.0 `db9527a`, and vLLM 0.30.0 `ced6857`. The 0.30 source checkout
    uses `VLLM_VERSION=0.30.0` because its generated `_version.py` is
    absent.
    - After rebase onto Ascend main
    `8a8e1e05ecb64a30804c99c8f362909f9a0cb5bf` (including the 0.31.0
    upgrade), the connector/lifecycle suites plus all of
    `tests/ut/kv_offload`: **1125 passed, 14 skipped per lane**, on verified
    vLLM main `c4df37d` and vLLM 0.31.0 `db9527a`.
    - Fixed the four CI failures by adding the missing error-mode fields to
    two existing test fixtures that use `__new__` and bypass constructors
    (two initialization lines). Adapted existing failure-state and
    worker-polling tests; **no new test functions or parametrized cases**.
    Existing block-error and real descriptor-to-transfer CPU tests remain
    enabled.
    - Additional submission-time runtime checks, kept outside the PR: eight
    layout/HMA combinations per version, checking deferred failure draining,
    completed/failed receive propagation, preservation of non-HMA invalid
    block IDs, and independence from the transfer-only HMA flag.
    - Full `bash format.sh ci` and `git diff --check` passed.
    - No NPU model/fault-injection rerun for this final conditional variant;
    the 14 existing skips and the mocked CPU transport are not end-to-end
    NPU coverage.
    
    - vLLM main:
    https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/commit/c4df37dfcc7f72ca41cd43801a468526ecf99a2b
    
